---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/shopify-email-vs-drip';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-drip');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/shopify-email-vs-drip');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Drip reach every shopper who adds to cart?",
    "answer": "No. Drip documents that Updated a Cart requires an identified person and enabled add-to-cart tracking. Anonymous add-to-cart sessions are not the same as eligible email recipients."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will the email always show the latest cart?",
    "answer": "Drip documents that items added after an email is queued are not included. Test quantity, variant and product changes in the actual workflow before promising exact cart contents."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both cart and checkout triggers be enabled?",
    "answer": "They can broaden the entry points, but check re-entry and purchase exits so one shopping session does not receive duplicate reminders."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the correct profit after the example discount?",
    "answer": "For the hypothetical $763 basket, product cost stays $213.64. A 12% discount leaves $671.44 revenue and $457.80 gross profit before other variable costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">Shopify Email vs Drip for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Decide whether earlier cart signals and customized email workflows justify moving beyond native Shopify recovery.</p>
    <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Compare triggers before comparing email editors</h2>
<p>A cart and an abandoned checkout describe different points in a purchase. Someone who adds a product and leaves before checkout may never enter a checkout-only flow. Start by listing the shoppers your current Shopify automation actually reaches, the content it sends, and the events that stop it. The native route is a useful baseline, not evidence that every earlier cart is covered.</p>
<p>This route keeps the Shopify Email name for continuity; current Shopify documentation places automations in Shopify Messaging. It offers browse, cart and checkout recovery over email, SMS and WhatsApp, with availability to check in your store. Earlier cart coverage alone is therefore not a reason to switch; compare the actual events and workflow control. Native abandoned-checkout email does not count toward the email allowance; text and WhatsApp are separately charged by recipient region. Use <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations/create">Shopify's current setup guide</a> when pricing the baseline.</p>
<h2>Inspect Drip's identified-cart event model</h2>
<p>Drip distinguishes Updated a Cart from Checkout Created/Updated. The former can arrive before checkout for an identified person; its integration needs add-to-cart tracking enabled. Its abandonment product block reads the latest cart event, but products added after an email is queued are not included. See <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/42835644176397-Shopify-Updated-a-Cart-vs-Checkout-Updated-Created-Events">Drip's event and cart-content guidance</a>.</p>
<p>Evaluate that event model against the native cart automation using the same known shopper. Drip is worth considering when your team needs workflow logic around those signals that its native setup cannot express. It does not make every anonymous visitor reachable. Count eligible identified carts separately from total add-to-cart sessions before forecasting the opportunity.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695514509-Abandoned-Cart-Workflow">Drip abandoned-cart workflow guide</a> provides an email recovery starting point. Customize it around the actual problem: a repeat customer may need a clear return link, while a new customer may need shipping or returns information. Do not assume a generic SMS branch exists because a comparison table lists SMS; this page evaluates the documented email workflow.</p>
<h2>Proposed test: cart changes while email is waiting</h2>
<ol><li>Enable the required tracking in a controlled setup. Use a known, eligible test contact to add variant A without starting checkout. Check the exact event received and whether the workflow enters once.</li><li>Add variant B, then proceed to checkout. Inspect cart and checkout events and the workflow's re-entry rules. Confirm that two triggers do not create two reminders for the same shopping session.</li><li>Change quantity or remove variant A after the email is queued. Inspect the delivered product block and return destination. The documented queue limitation means you should not promise that every last-minute cart edit will appear.</li><li>Purchase before a later email, then check the exit rule and pending sends. Repeat with an unsubscribed contact to verify exclusion.</li></ol>
<p>This is a proposed acceptance test, not a report of observed performance. Save event payloads, queue timestamps and actual message content. If stale contents are unacceptable for your catalog, change the workflow or content expectations before moving the audience.</p>
<h2>Keep Shopify when checkout recovery is the real job</h2>
<p>If nearly all reachable abandoners already reach checkout and your native message is adequate, another event pipeline may add little practical value. First inspect native send failures and payment events using <a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/abandoned-checkouts">Shopify's recovery guide</a>. A payment failure calls for assistance, not an assumed discount. Retain working native recovery until Drip's required events, links and exits pass the test.</p>
<p>At cutover, pause the overlapping native recovery workflow before enabling the replacement for that audience. Transfer subscription status and suppression history, not just email addresses. Rebuild any custom segment from documented fields, and compare a sample of included and excluded customers. Do not use parallel live sends as a migration test.</p>
<h2>Price the reachable audience and the incentive separately</h2>
<p>Request a Drip estimate for the actual eligible audience, expected growth and required features; compare it with the native email and channel bill plus operating time. The number of site visits is not the same as the number of people who can enter a Drip email flow. Include inactive records and suppression treatment in the quote discussion rather than assuming all stored contacts cost or behave the same way.</p>
<p>Hypothetical basket math: $763 at a 72% full-price gross margin implies $213.64 fixed product cost. A 12% code removes $91.56 of revenue, leaving $671.44 revenue and $457.80 gross profit, before shipping, payment, returns and message costs. Full-price gross profit is $549.36. Multiplying that profit by 88% would incorrectly reduce the product cost too.</p>
<p>For an earlier-cart pilot, split eligible identified pre-checkout abandoners into comparable treatment and no-recovery groups where feasible. Keep existing checkout treatment consistent across groups so it does not masquerade as Drip lift. Count unique orders and net contribution after incentives, refunds and the extra platform cost. Choose the experiment duration for your traffic; a universal holdout percentage or fixed two-week window cannot guarantee a useful result.</p>
<h2>Choose based on the missing workflow</h2>
<p>Use native Shopify recovery when checkout coverage and its available customization meet the need. Evaluate Drip when its event model or custom email branching fills a verified gap in your native setup and the team will maintain that logic. Sequenzy remains another supplier to assess against the same event and exclusion requirements, not a proven superior layer or a prerequisite for either option.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Recovery decision FAQ" />
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
